Chapter: E1000RingWrap
  cites Foreword chapter Maybe
  cites Kernel chapter E1000e
  cites Kernel chapter Pci

 Both rings hold sixteen descriptors and until this test nothing in the
 tree drove either past its end: e1000-bringup injects one frame and
 sends one, dhcp-acquire-e1000 exchanges a handful. So the two places
 that decide a wrap -- the int-mod in e1000-recycle-rx and the one in
 e1000-send-at -- had no arm that could tell a correct answer from an
 absent one. Forty of each against sixteen is two full turns and part of
 a third.

 The byte total is here rather than a count alone because the count is
 satisfied by forty frames of anything. e1000-recycle-rx clears the
 status and length words and deliberately does NOT rewrite the
 descriptor's buffer address; the model re-reads that address on every
 placement, so an address damaged on the way round the ring moves this
 total and nothing else would notice.

 Both halves were measured against a deliberately broken driver on
 2026-08-03, because an arm that cannot fail is not evidence. Dropping
 the int-mod in e1000-recycle-rx gave count 16, wrapped no, bytes 63808,
 and moved no other row. Dropping the one in e1000-send-at left every
 receive row standing and gave send failed 24. Neither sabotage was
 submitted; both rows discriminate.

Section: Fixtures

 Two and a half turns of a sixteen-entry ring.

  wrap-count : Integer = 40

  bring-up : PciScanResult -> E1000Device
  bring-up (scan) =
    when e1000-find scan
      is Just (d) -> e1000-init d
      is None -> e1000-absent

  mk-frame : Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  mk-frame (i) (n) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc else mk-frame (i + 1) n (list-push acc i)

Section: Judges

  yn : Boolean -> Text
  yn (b) = if b then "yes" else "no"

  WrapTally = record {
    w-count : Integer,
    w-bytes : Integer,
    w-odd : Integer
  }

  sum-bytes : List Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  sum-bytes (l) (i) (n) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc else sum-bytes l (i + 1) n (acc + list-at l i)

 The fuel is the ring size times the frame count, which is far above any
 honest run and still terminates a driver that reports a frame forever.

  drain-fuel : Integer = 640

  drain : E1000Device, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> WrapTally
  drain (d) (fuel) (count) (bytes) (odd) =
    if fuel <= 0 then WrapTally { w-count = count, w-bytes = bytes, w-odd = odd }
    else let r = e1000-poll-frame d
    in if (r.r-has-frame) == False
       then WrapTally { w-count = count, w-bytes = bytes, w-odd = odd }
       else let f = r.r-frame
       in let n = list-length f
       in let total = bytes + sum-bytes f 0 n 0
       in let odd-next = if n == 60 then odd else odd + 1
       in drain d (fuel - 1) (count + 1) total odd-next

  send-all : E1000Device, List Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  send-all (d) (frame) (i) (bad) =
    if i >= wrap-count then bad
    else let bad-next = if e1000-send-frame d frame == 1 then bad else bad + 1
    in send-all d frame (i + 1) bad-next

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    let d = bring-up (pci-scan-bus 0)
    in let t = drain d drain-fuel 0 0 0
    in let after = e1000-poll-frame d
    in let bad = send-all d (mk-frame 0 64 []) 0 0
    in act
      print-line-uni ("present      : " & yn (d.e-present))
      print-line-uni ("ring size    : " & show e1000-rx-count)
      print-line-uni ("recv count   : " & show (t.w-count))
      print-line-uni ("recv wrapped : " & yn ((t.w-count) > e1000-rx-count))
      print-line-uni ("recv bytes   : " & show (t.w-bytes))
      print-line-uni ("odd lengths  : " & show (t.w-odd))
      print-line-uni ("poll after   : " & yn (after.r-has-frame))
      print-line-uni ("send count   : " & show wrap-count)
      print-line-uni ("send failed  : " & show bad)
    end
  end

